text 11,1.5 "How a neural network learns"
say "Let's start with the shape of the thing. It's just layers of small units."

text 4.5,4.5 "Input"
box 5,7 3,2.5 "x1"
box 5,12 3,2.5 "x2"
box 5,17 3,2.5 "x3"
say "On the left, your inputs. Three numbers describing one example."

text 16.5,2.5 "Hidden"
box 17,5 3,2.5 "h1"
box 17,10 3,2.5 "h2"
box 17,15 3,2.5 "h3"
box 17,20 3,2.5 "h4"
say "In the middle, a hidden layer. These are the units that learn features."

text 28.5,9.5 "Output"
box 29,12 3.5,2.5 "y"
say "On the right, one output. The prediction."

stroke 8,8.25 17,6.25
stroke 8,8.25 17,11.25
stroke 8,8.25 17,16.25
stroke 8,8.25 17,21.25
stroke 8,13.25 17,6.25
stroke 8,13.25 17,11.25
stroke 8,13.25 17,16.25
stroke 8,13.25 17,21.25
stroke 8,18.25 17,6.25
stroke 8,18.25 17,11.25
stroke 8,18.25 17,16.25
stroke 8,18.25 17,21.25
say "Every input connects to every hidden unit. That's the mesh."

stroke 20,6.25 29,13.25
stroke 20,11.25 29,13.25
stroke 20,16.25 29,13.25
stroke 20,21.25 29,13.25
say "And every hidden unit feeds the output."
# --- correction: "Hidden" ran into the title. Clear the band and rewrite both.
erase 18,2.2 3 "Rubbing out that label -- it was crowding the title."
text 11,1.5 "How a neural network learns"
text 17,3.8 "Hidden"

# --- focus: pick one unit to open up
circle 18.5,11.25 3 "Circling h2 here -- this is the one we'll open up."
say "Every one of these units does the same small job. Let's open up h2."
# --- repair: that erase clipped the top off h1. Redraw it.
box 17,5 3,2.5 "h1"
say "Oops, I rubbed out the top of h1 there. Putting it back."

# --- the zoom: what one unit actually does
text 1.5,23.3 "Inside h2: multiply, add, squash"
say "Here is the whole job of a single unit. Three steps, that's all."

text 3.5,25.2 "x1"
text 3.5,26.6 "x2"
text 3.5,28 "x3"
arrow 5.5,25.2 12.6,26 "w1"
arrow 5.5,26.6 12.6,26.5 "w2"
arrow 5.5,28 12.6,27 "w3"
say "Each incoming number is multiplied by a weight. The weights are what the network learns."

box 13,25 4.5,3 "sum"
say "Add all three products together."

arrow 17.5,26.5 21,26.5
box 21,25 5.5,3 "ReLU"
say "Then squash the result. ReLU just means: if it's negative, make it zero."

arrow 26.5,26.5 31,26.5
text 31.5,26.5 "h2"
say "And that number is what h2 passes forward."
# tie the prediction to the output box before we clear anything
arrow 32.6,13.25 33.6,13.25
text 34,11.5 "guess"
text 34,13.25 "0.7"
text 34,15 "truth"
text 34,16.7 "1.0"
say "Say the network guesses 0.7, but the right answer was 1.0."

# --- wipe the zoom. One rectangle now that erase takes w,h.
erase 0,22.8 40,8 "Wiping the close-up now -- we need the room."
say "We're done with the close-up, so let me wipe it and use the space."

text 1.5,24.3 "Learning: compare, then push the error backwards"
box 2,26 5,2.8 "guess 0.7"
arrow 7,27.4 9.5,27.4
box 9.5,26 5,2.8 "true 1.0"
arrow 14.5,27.4 17,27.4
box 17,26 6,2.8 "error 0.3"
say "Subtract them. That gap, 0.3, is the error."

arrow 23,27.4 25.5,27.4
box 25.5,26 9,2.8 "nudge the weights"
say "Now every weight that pushed the guess too low gets nudged up a little."

arrow 28,25.8 21,23.2 "backwards"
say "That nudge travels back through the network, layer by layer. That's backpropagation."
say "Do this a few thousand times and the weights stop being random. That's training."
